page('01 · EXECUTIVE SUMMARY','Make the next customer action visible',lead('Overhead LLC develops OverHead, a Windows-centered workspace for the office work that follows a customer inquiry: quotes, invoices, reminders, staff handoffs, and approvals.')+'''
<p>The business addresses a practical management problem. When customer information and pending work sit across inboxes, spreadsheets, notes, and payment tools, the owner must reconstruct what happened and decide what should happen next. OverHead aims to make that work visible, assign responsibility, and retain the context needed to finish it.</p>
<h2>The commercial approach</h2><p>Begin with owner-led service businesses that have a Windows office workstation and recurring quote or payment follow-up. Sell a focused improvement to an existing workflow, supported by assisted setup and measurable before-and-after results. Keep a broad product architecture while concentrating early selling and onboarding on two repeatable use cases.</p>
'''+cards([('Published desktop pricing','$59–$299 / month','Silver, Gold, and Black workspace plans; paid activation remains a launch gate.'),('Product stage','Built; validation ahead','Desktop release, website, documentation, and operating workflows exist. Paid traction has not been verified.')])+'''
<h2>Near-term priorities</h2><p>Complete production billing, signed distribution, and customer recovery tests; recruit 15 pilot offices; validate first-week use and willingness to pay; then build a repeatable acquisition and retention process. Treat Cylinder, the separate server/IoT edition, as a later commercial option with its own delivery requirements.</p>
'''+table(['Illustrative base case','Year 1','Year 2','Year 3'],[['Year-end paid workspaces']+[num(a['ending']) for a in A],['Annual net revenue']+[money(a['revenue']) for a in A],['Operating profit / (loss)']+[money(a['operating_profit']) for a in A]])+note('The model assumes $175,000 of new opening capital, a $126.50 monthly plan mix, and 2% monthly customer churn. These are planning inputs, not a funding request, company valuation, existing cash balance, or forecast supported by current sales. Base-case operating profit becomes sustainably positive in month 15.')+cite('P1','P2','P3','M1'))

page('02 · COMPANY OVERVIEW','Overhead LLC',lead('Business name: Overhead LLC. DBA: Overhead LLC. Product name: OverHead.')+'''
<h2>Mission</h2><p>Make daily office operations clearer, more accountable, and accessible to owner-led businesses.</p>
<h2>Business description</h2><p>Overhead LLC is developing and commercializing business administration software. Its primary offer is a recurring workspace subscription. The product combines a local Windows application with a shared account and browser layer so the office can organize customer work, manage staff responsibilities, and preserve operational history.</p>
<p>The business will operate as a lean software company, with product development, support, and sales coordinated by the founder and supplemented by specialist contractors. A remote operating model is assumed for budgeting; a permanent office lease is not included.</p>
'''+table(['Business element','Plan position'],[['Primary customers','Owner-led local and service businesses; initial selling focus on small office teams.'],['Initial geography','United States. Raleigh/Triangle and North Carolina are proposed pilot markets, not a statement of incorporation or registered address.'],['Distribution','Public website and Windows download; guided onboarding and partner referrals.'],['Revenue model','Monthly desktop workspace subscriptions; separate Cylinder deployment option.'],['Current evidence','Version 0.3.12 in project metadata and an available public desktop ZIP; published product and user-guide pages.'],['Ownership and formation','Business name/DBA confirmed by the owner. Ownership percentages, formation jurisdiction, registered address, and capitalization were not supplied.']])+'''
<h2>Operating principles</h2><p>Make the next action clear. Keep the owner in control of sensitive decisions. Minimize unnecessary data. Help customers adopt one useful workflow at a time. Provide recovery and export paths. Describe product and commercial capabilities accurately.</p>
<h2>Governance</h2><p>Maintain company-level accounting, written contractor/IP assignments, a controlled release process, and a monthly operating review. Document who can commit spending, approve access changes, sign agreements, and authorize customer refunds before those responsibilities are delegated.</p>
'''+cite('P1','P2','P4'))

page('03 · CUSTOMER PROBLEM','The cost of unfinished follow-through',lead('The target customer already has work coming in. The office needs a reliable way to move that work toward a completed service, a paid invoice, and the next customer interaction.')+'''
'''+table(['What the office experiences','Business consequence','OverHead response'],[['Quotes sit without a next step','Slow decisions and avoidable lost opportunities','A visible quote status, responsible person, and scheduled follow-up.'],['Payment information is scattered','Time spent chasing context and overdue invoices','An invoice work queue and provider/payment reference context.'],['The owner is the routing system','Staff wait; decisions stay in the owner’s head','Defined tasks, roles, approvals, and handoff records.'],['Customer history is incomplete','Repeated questions and inconsistent service','Customer records and a shared operating playbook.'],['A problem cannot be reconstructed','Support delays and customer disagreement','Reviewable activity, support exports, and recovery tools.']])+'''
<h2>The first offer: an office follow-through review</h2><p>Use the existing “Office Revenue Leak Check” concept as a discovery session. Review a small, permissioned sample of open quotes, unpaid invoices, waiting approvals, and customers with no next action. Produce an owner-approved worklist and establish a baseline. Do not describe all open amounts as money that OverHead will recover.</p>
<h2>A measurable value test</h2><p>For each pilot, compare a two-week baseline with four weeks of use. Track administrative minutes, overdue next actions, quote response times, and completed follow-ups. Record payment outcomes separately from software use; customer payment timing has multiple causes.</p>
'''+note('<b>Illustrative value calculation:</b> at an assumed $35 per administrative hour, saving four hours per month is worth $140 of staff capacity. Against a $129 Gold subscription, that is $11 of monthly capacity value above the fee, before setup costs. A stronger target is eight hours, or $280 of capacity value. These are examples to test, not measured customer savings or guaranteed cash savings.')+'''
<p>A customer should buy because a recurring operational problem is solved at an acceptable cost. Broad feature counts and AI language are supporting details; retention depends on whether the office keeps using the workflow.</p>
'''+cite('P1','P2'))

